WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.869 --> 00:00:03.911
A post-mortem, also called an incident review,

2
00:00:04.154 --> 00:00:07.091
is a structured discussion after a major outage.

3
00:00:07.791 --> 00:00:09.765
The goal: understanding what happened,

4
00:00:09.881 --> 00:00:12.992
why, and what changes so it does not come back.

5
00:00:13.989 --> 00:00:18.796
The result is a short document with a timeline, causes and actions with an owner.

6
00:00:19.785 --> 00:00:24.545
Google made the approach widely known in the book Site Reliability Engineering,

7
00:00:24.685 --> 00:00:28.481
from twenty sixteen, with a chapter on postmortem culture.

8
00:00:29.799 --> 00:00:32.040
The core is that the discussion is blameless:

9
00:00:32.179 --> 00:00:35.012
you look for what in the system made the mistake possible,

10
00:00:35.151 --> 00:00:36.265
not for who made it.

11
00:00:37.384 --> 00:00:41.134
Someone who pressed the wrong button did so because the button was there,

12
00:00:41.343 --> 00:00:43.909
the procedure was unclear or it was busy.

13
00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.244
That is not the same as no accountability.

14
00:00:47.897 --> 00:00:50.974
Whoever deliberately breaks rules is held to account.

15
00:00:52.016 --> 00:00:55.174
But an ordinary slip is information, not guilt.

16
00:00:56.635 --> 00:00:59.863
A timeline is the backbone: minute by minute what happened,

17
00:00:59.978 --> 00:01:01.952
what people saw and what they did.

18
00:01:03.043 --> 00:01:08.464
Then you look for causes, for example with the 5 Whys: asking why again and again,

19
00:01:08.662 --> 00:01:10.996
until you reach something you can change.

20
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.868
Taiichi Ohno made the method known at Toyota.

21
00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:21.088
Careful: complex outages often have several contributing causes, not one root.

22
00:01:22.436 --> 00:01:23.702
Two ITIL words help.

23
00:01:24.475 --> 00:01:27.354
A workaround is a temporary way to keep working,

24
00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:29.966
while the real cause is not yet solved.

25
00:01:31.006 --> 00:01:34.315
For example, restarting the link by hand every morning.

26
00:01:35.335 --> 00:01:40.073
A known error is a problem whose cause is known, but that has not been solved yet.

27
00:01:41.129 --> 00:01:42.836
You record it with the workaround,

28
00:01:42.893 --> 00:01:46.968
so the service desk knows straight away what to do at the next report.

29
00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:51.755
What experts argue about: how many post-mortems do you do?

30
00:01:52.444 --> 00:01:55.126
For every outage it costs too much time.

31
00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.245
Only for the biggest, you miss patterns.

32
00:01:59.268 --> 00:02:03.077
Many teams agree a threshold, for example for priority one,

33
00:02:03.192 --> 00:02:06.629
or for every outage that stops a line for more than an hour.

34
00:02:07.638 --> 00:02:12.236
The weakest point is usually the follow-up: actions without an owner and date.

35
00:02:13.324 --> 00:02:17.155
A sharp question: which actions from the last post-mortem are done?

36
00:02:18.452 --> 00:02:19.404
An example.

37
00:02:20.104 --> 00:02:22.995
At a soft-drinks manufacturer the MES,

38
00:02:23.122 --> 00:02:26.396
the system that tracks orders and production on the floor,

39
00:02:26.571 --> 00:02:29.230
was down for three hours on Saturday morning.

40
00:02:29.936 --> 00:02:31.828
You facilitate the post-mortem.

41
00:02:32.818 --> 00:02:33.584
The timeline.

42
00:02:34.303 --> 00:02:38.169
At ten to six, a scheduled renewal of the server’s certificate.

43
00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:41.595
At six, the morning shift cannot log in.

44
00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:45.531
At twenty past six the on-call team is phoned.

45
00:02:46.351 --> 00:02:51.065
At twenty to eight the cause is found, and at nine everything works again.

46
00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.938
Why one: why could nobody log in?

47
00:02:54.625 --> 00:02:58.433
The new certificate was not recognised by the scanners on the line.

48
00:02:59.399 --> 00:03:01.361
Why two: why was it not recognised?

49
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.037
The scanners had an old list of trusted certificates.

50
00:03:06.047 --> 00:03:06.639
Now you.

51
00:03:07.303 --> 00:03:11.947
Continue with why three and four, name a workaround and two actions with an owner.

52
00:03:12.653 --> 00:03:13.686
Think for a moment.

53
00:03:15.055 --> 00:03:17.017
Why three: why was that list old?

54
00:03:17.784 --> 00:03:22.591
The scanners fall under OT, and were never included in the IT updates.

55
00:03:23.618 --> 00:03:24.837
Why four: why not?

56
00:03:25.632 --> 00:03:29.510
There is no overview of which devices depend on which certificates,

57
00:03:29.696 --> 00:03:32.471
and IT and OT plan their changes separately.

58
00:03:33.428 --> 00:03:38.154
The workaround: at a certificate change, first update the list on the scanners.

59
00:03:39.179 --> 00:03:40.108
Two actions.

60
00:03:40.842 --> 00:03:45.161
The OT administrator makes an overview of the dependencies within a month.

61
00:03:45.851 --> 00:03:49.972
The IT manager brings OT into the change meeting from now on.

62
00:03:50.998 --> 00:03:51.996
And no culprit:

63
00:03:52.217 --> 00:03:56.489
the administrator who did the update at ten to six followed the procedure.
